{"name":"Ice Drop","hex":"#d3e2ee","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"ice drop","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Frozen fruit juice, flavored sugar water or the like, on a stick, of a size to be one serving; a popsicle.","The small vesicles, filled with fluid, occurring on the stems and leaves of certain plants of the family Aizoaceae."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/ice_drop"}],"entries":[{"title":"Ice","blocks":[{"text":"Ice is water that is frozen into a solid state, typically forming at or below temperatures of 0 °C, 32 °F, or 273.15 K. It occurs naturally on Earth, on other planets, in Oort cloud objects, and as interstellar ice. As a naturally occurring crystalline inorganic solid with an ordered structure, ice is considered to be a mineral. Depending on the presence of impurities such as particles of soil or bubbles of air, it can appear transparent or a more or less opaque bluish-white color."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/67/Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg/330px-Ice_Block%2C_Canal_Park%2C_Duluth_%2832752478892%29.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ice","wikidata":"Q23392"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Ice_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png/500px-Ice_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Ice_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png/1280px-Ice_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c9/Ice_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png/500px-Ice_Ih_Crystal_Lattice.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The three-dimensional crystal structure of H2O ice Ih (c) is composed of bases of H2O ice molecules (b) loc